WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.315
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:15.135
Unit twenty: Big Questions in Philosophy. Today you will use noun clauses with 'What … is …'.

4
00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:18.192
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.331
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:24.254
Philosophy.

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.352
Philosophy means the study of big questions about life.

8
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.124
Philosophy asks why we exist.

9
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.125
Your turn. Say: Philosophy.

10
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:37.910
Philosophy.

11
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.523
Question.

12
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:43.457
Question means something you ask to find an answer.

13
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.527
That is a deep question.

14
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:48.792
Your turn. Say: Question.

15
00:00:51.633 --> 00:00:52.437
Question.

16
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:54.126
Argument.

17
00:00:55.117 --> 00:00:57.766
Argument means reasons that support an idea.

18
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.454
Her argument was very convincing.

19
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.435
Your turn. Say: Argument.

20
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.139
Argument.

21
00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:08.699
Reason.

22
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:12.519
Reason means logical thinking, or a cause.

23
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:15.546
Use reason, not only feelings.

24
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.640
Your turn. Say: Reason.

25
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.420
Reason.

26
00:01:23.183 --> 00:01:23.975
Belief.

27
00:01:24.983 --> 00:01:27.192
Belief means something you think is true.

28
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:29.597
People have different beliefs.

29
00:01:30.183 --> 00:01:32.557
Your turn. Say: Belief.

30
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.122
Belief.

31
00:01:36.883 --> 00:01:37.826
Ethics.

32
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:41.443
Ethics means ideas about right and wrong.

33
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:43.957
Ethics guides our choices.

34
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:46.970
Your turn. Say: Ethics.

35
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:50.788
Ethics.

36
00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.342
Debate.

37
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.244
Debate means a formal discussion with two sides.

38
00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.838
We had a debate about happiness.

39
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.893
Your turn. Say: Debate.

40
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:05.769
Debate.

41
00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:07.448
Wisdom.

42
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.995
Wisdom means deep knowledge and good judgment.

43
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:13.258
Grandparents share wisdom.

44
00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.224
Your turn. Say: Wisdom.

45
00:02:19.067 --> 00:02:19.928
Wisdom.

46
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:24.744
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:27.339
Visal, what is a good life?

48
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.156
What makes a good life is different for everyone.

49
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.565
What matters to you?

50
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.588
What matters most to me is family and kindness.

51
00:02:40.683 --> 00:02:41.975
And what is wisdom?

52
00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:46.650
What I believe is that wisdom comes from listening.

53
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.637
Part three: grammar.

54
00:02:50.483 --> 00:02:53.621
Grammar focus: Noun clauses (What is).

55
00:02:53.921 --> 00:02:55.212
Look at the pattern.

56
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:58.497
What makes a good life is different for everyone.

57
00:02:59.383 --> 00:03:01.511
What matters most is kindness.

58
00:03:02.417 --> 00:03:04.695
What I believe is that people can change.

59
00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:11.095
A 'What' clause can be the subject. It takes a singular verb: What makes a good life is…

60
00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:16.151
Be careful! Don't say: What makes a good life are different for everyone.

61
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.237
Say: What makes a good life is different for everyone.

62
00:03:21.537 --> 00:03:23.850
A 'what' clause takes a singular verb.

63
00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:27.389
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.317
Thinking Deeply.

65
00:03:29.617 --> 00:03:32.464
Philosophy asks big questions about life.

66
00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:36.151
What makes a good life is different for everyone.

67
00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:40.699
Philosophers use reason and argument, not only belief.

68
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.059
Ethics helps us decide what is right.

69
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:47.031
What we gain from debate is wisdom.

70
00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:52.205
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

71
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.723
What makes a good life blank different for everyone.

72
00:03:56.023 --> 00:03:59.363
A, is. B, are. C, be.

73
00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.679
The answer is A: is.

74
00:04:05.817 --> 00:04:08.083
Ideas about right and wrong are blank.

75
00:04:08.383 --> 00:04:11.990
A, ethics. B, beliefs. C, debates.

76
00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:17.533
The answer is A: ethics.

77
00:04:18.683 --> 00:04:21.124
Deep knowledge and good judgment is blank.

78
00:04:21.424 --> 00:04:24.729
A, wisdom. B, reason. C, argument.

79
00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:30.231
The answer is A: wisdom.

80
00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:34.504
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

81
00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.593
What big question do you often think about?

82
00:04:46.383 --> 00:04:47.965
Now listen to Visal.

83
00:04:48.265 --> 00:04:56.446
I often think about happiness. What makes people happy is not money, I believe. What really matters is good relationships.

84
00:04:56.746 --> 00:04:58.432
Excellent answer, Visal!

85
00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:03.467
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun clauses (what is).

86
00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:08.590
Today's value is wisdom. Think carefully and listen to good advice before you decide.

87
00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.577
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

88
00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:14.460
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

